{
  "ranking": [
    {
      "id": "patch_a3e524527915",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 17439,
      "mechanism": "The failing trace shows o_wb_ack asserting one cycle early (cycle 6) and then oscillating incorrectly (cycles 7, 11, 15, 19, 24, 25). The current logic assigns o_wb_ack <= wb_stb every cycle, where wb_stb is combinatorial (i_wb_stb && !o_wb_stall). This makes ack follow the raw wishbone strobe without cycle qualification. The patch inserts a one-cycle delay gated by i_wb_cyc, ensuring ack only rises on the cycle after a qualified bus grant, matching the cycle-exact protocol."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_f8d57d590691",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 17439,
      "mechanism": "Same o_wb_ack misalignment as above. This patch qualifies the ack directly with the bus-cycle grant (i_wb_cyc) instead of adding a register delay. It forces ack to be the conjunction of wb_stb and i_wb_cyc, which suppresses the early/extra assertions seen at cycles 6, 11, 15, 19, 24 when the bus grant is not yet active."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_c1fed5d49f35",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 9010,
      "mechanism": "The cmd state machine enters the first command cycle (3'h0) unconditionally on pre_cmd_state. If pre_cmd_state is not yet asserted, the machine still advances r_cmd_state and drives ll_cmd_stb early, which in turn pulls r_cmd_busy high and asserts o_wb_ack prematurely. Guarding the first state transition with pre_cmd_state aligns command start to the qualified cycle and prevents the early o_wb_ack at cycle 6."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_2643820b9fc0",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 17319,
      "mechanism": "The trace shows o_wb_ack cycling on/off with a period that mirrors the SPI byte serialization (cycles 11, 15, 19, 24, 25). The current o_wb_data assignment reads FIFO bytes in big-endian order (mem_3, mem_2, mem_1, mem_0). If the bus expects little-endian byte order, the FIFO readback will be misinterpreted as command completion tokens, causing r_cmd_busy to drop and o_wb_ack to deassert. Reversing the byte concatenation to little-endian matches the bus expectation and stabilizes ack."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_6083bec9b350",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 9715,
      "mechanism": "At r_cmd_state == 3'h5 the code unconditionally checks r_have_resp and may set ll_fifo_rd. When r_use_fifo is asserted (FIFO write command), the response is a write-status token, not a data token, so r_have_resp should not gate the transition. The missing !r_use_fifo qualifier lets the state machine advance prematurely during FIFO writes, dropping r_cmd_busy and o_wb_ack at cycles 15-25. Adding the qualifier keeps the machine in the correct wait state."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_afa0971cd501",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 9715,
      "mechanism": "Complementary to patch_6083bec9b350: the rsp-state machine enters SDSPI_RSP_GETTOKEN at r_cmd_state == 3'h5 only when r_have_resp is true. Without this guard, the response-state machine can jump to token-wait during FIFO writes where no data-response token exists, causing r_rsp_state to reach RDCOMPLETE early and deassert r_cmd_busy/o_wb_ack. The guard aligns the response machine to the actual token arrival."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_436fc440e456",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 9603,
      "mechanism": "At r_cmd_state == 3'h4 (CRC byte sent) the machine currently does nothing until r_have_resp. The trace shows the first response token arriving while still in state 3'h4 (cycle 11). Qualifying this state with !r_have_resp makes the machine explicitly wait for the token here, preventing premature advance to state 3'h5 and the resulting o_wb_ack glitch at cycle 11."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_e69de1c27f07",
      "failure_port": "o_wb_ack",
      "source_line": 9969,
      "mechanism": "During FIFO read (r_use_fifo && r_fifo_wr), the cmd machine at state 3'h6 transmits FIFO bytes while ll_fifo_rd_complete is low. The trace shows o_wb_ack dropping at cycle 15 when the last FIFO byte is sent but ll_fifo_rd_complete has not yet asserted. Adding the !ll_fifo_rd_complete guard keeps ll_cmd_stb asserted until the low-level signals completion, holding r_cmd_busy and o_wb_ack high for the full FIFO transfer."
    }
  ]
  ,
  "explanation": "All cited failures center on o_wb_ack asserting at cycle 6 (expected 0), then toggling at cycles 7, 11, 15, 19, 24, 25. The root cause is premature deassertion of r_cmd_busy, which is driven by the command/response state machines and the ack generation logic. The top two patches (a3e524527915, f8d57d590691) directly fix the ack generation to honor the bus-cycle grant. The next three (c1fed5d49f35, 2643820b9fc0, 6083bec9b350) prevent the command machine from starting or advancing early due to missing qualifiers on pre_cmd_state, FIFO byte order, and r_use_fifo. The last three (afa0971cd501, 436fc440e456, e69de1c27f07) align the response machine and FIFO-read completion gating so r_cmd_busy stays high for the exact cycle-exact duration the trace expects."
}
